WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.520
- Thanks to Curiosity Stream

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.701
for keeping LegalEagle in the air.

00:00:03.701 --> 00:00:06.480
(sword clangs)
- I wish had enough poison

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.020
for the whole pack of you.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:13.020
I would gladly give my life
to watch you all swallow it.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:14.250
- I haven't seen that in court,

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.114
but generally, that's
not a great strategy.

00:00:17.114 --> 00:00:19.864
(cheerful music)

00:00:20.759 --> 00:00:23.600
Hey Legal Eagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.850
Today we are covering the trial

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.350
of Tyrion from Game of Thrones.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:29.470
Like a lot of you out there,

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.720
I am waiting for the series finale.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.860
But in the meantime,
let's take a look back

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:36.320
at one of the more famous repercussions

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.670
of the Purple Wedding, which is the trial

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.820
of Tyrion Lannister, who is framed

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.677
for the murder of King Joffrey.

00:00:45.677 --> 00:00:46.760
(Joffrey gasps)

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:47.630
- He's choking!

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.410
- It's been a long time
since I've seen this,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.220
so I'll be looking at it with fresh eyes,

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:52.510
but it'll be interesting to see

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.360
how realistic the trial scene is

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.890
compared to American modern jurisprudence.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.920
As always, leave your comments in the form

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.690
of an objection, which I will
either sustain or overrule,

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:04.490
and stick around until
the end of the video,

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:08.260
where I give Game of Thrones
a grade for legal realism.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.100
So, without further ado, let's dig in

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.750
to the trial of Tyrion Lannister.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.030
So, here Tyrion is being held in captivity

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:23.700
by the equivalent of the
police in King's Landing.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.630
To recap, King Joffrey was
killed on his wedding day

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.990
by some sort of gruesome poison,

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:32.380
and because of many of the
statements that Tyrion made

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.860
to and about the king, he is being accused

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.720
of actually killing King Joffrey,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.820
which, of course, is not in fact true.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.380
Nonetheless, he's being held in captivity

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.523
and is being accused of
the crime of regicide.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.810
All right, the sort of Game of Thrones

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:53.810
equivalent of the perp walk,
being walked into court.

00:01:54.225 --> 00:01:55.153
- [Man] King slayer!

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.340
- So, this is a pretty unusual court.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.120
All of the people in the audience,

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.670
generally called the
gallery, almost always you

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.550
have it being perpendicular to the

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.140
actual place where the judge sits.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.410
Here, you have these long rows

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:15.910
going toward where the
judge is going to sit.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:18.820
This doesn't make a whole
lot of sense as a courtroom.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:21.210
Obviously, this is a
great hall where usually

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.410
official business is conducted.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:25.260
But this doesn't make a whole
lotta sense as a courtroom.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.240
Generally speaking, the accused don't have

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.380
to wear handcuffs when they're in court,

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.010
unless there is some known reason

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.520
why they have to be restrained,

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.670
like they are known to
have outbursts in court

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:48.680
or they jump across council table

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.760
and try and attack the
judge, the prosecutor,

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.050
which has been known to happen.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.590
But otherwise, for obvious reasons,

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.780
you don't want to poison
the mind of the jury

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:58.640
and see this person as some sort

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.210
of animal that has to be chained up.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:01.860
You want them to get a fair hearing.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.080
So generally, criminal defendants are not

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.680
locked up when they're in court.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.650
You would generally rise when
the judge arrives as well.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.753
- I, Tommen of the House Baratheon,

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.000
First of My Name, King of
the Andals and the First Men

00:03:21.973 --> 00:03:24.760
and Lord of the Seven Kingdoms, do hereby

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:27.273
recuse myself from this trial.

00:03:28.478 --> 00:03:30.940
Tywin of the House
Lannister, Hand of the King

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:35.060
and Protector of the Realm,
will sit as judge in my stead.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.610
- So, in the US, you have
a constitutional right

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.620
to a jury trial, and that jury

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:41.310
must be made up of your peers.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:45.510
Now, query in the sort of royal house

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.980
who would be the peers that would sit

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:52.760
in judgment over a nobleman
like Tyrion Lannister.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.510
But you can also see that it
is a clear conflict of interest

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:00.100
for one of the three
members of the panel or jury

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:01.880
that's going to decide your fate

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:03.690
to be a member of your own house.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.030
Here you can imagine
prejudice going both ways.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:07.880
There could be prejudice
because his father

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:11.340
may want him to face execution

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.800
because of how much he loved
Joffrey, his grandchild.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.770
Or you could imagine that
he could get leniency

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:19.980
because he's a member of the same family.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.580
- Tyrion of the House Lannister,

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:25.593
you stand accused by the
queen regent of regicide.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:29.393
Did you kill King Joffrey?

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:32.710
- No.

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:35.561
- Did your wife, the Lady Sansa?

00:04:35.561 --> 00:04:37.280
- Not that I know of.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:38.150
- That's a fair response.

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:42.770
He may not know whether his
wife committed homicide or not.

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:43.690
You would have to ask her.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.640
So that would be speculation for him

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:49.037
to talk about whether his
wife killed King Joffrey.

00:04:49.037 --> 00:04:50.913
- How would you say he died, then?

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.673
- Choked on his pigeon pie.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.430
- So you would blame the bakers?

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:58.380
- Or the pigeons.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.190
Just leave me out of it.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:01.820
- I should note here
that there are already

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:04.450
several problems from an
American law perspective.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.630
Number one, if this was in America,

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.380
Tyrion would be given the right

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:10.740
against self-incrimination
under the Fifth Amendment,

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:13.460
which would allow him
to not have to testify

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:15.970
against himself in a criminal proceeding.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:20.120
And already, the adjudicator
has asked him several questions

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.340
that could be incriminating
towards his defense,

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:26.000
and he would have the
right not to take the stand

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.610
and not to have to answer those questions.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:28.980
But I don't get the sense that

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:32.410
Tyrion could have avoided those
questions if he wanted to.

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.680
Additionally, Tyrion would
have a Sixth Amendment right

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:38.700
to an attorney being present
throughout this entire ordeal

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:41.830
because we don't expect
members of the public

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:44.730
to be able to defend
themselves in a court of law

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.220
without the help of an
advocate like a lawyer.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:51.650
And here, he has no attorney
to act as an intermediary,

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.330
and he makes these statements
that are somewhat inflammatory

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:57.090
and could get him into trouble.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:00.270
- The crown may call its first witness.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:01.970
- Once we got King Joffrey safely away

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:04.480
from the mob, the Imp rounded on him.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.680
He slapped the king across the face

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.028
and called him a vicious idiot and a fool.

00:06:09.028 --> 00:06:10.650
(audience gasps)

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:13.090
It wasn't the first time
the Imp threatened Joffrey.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:14.830
Right here in this throne room,

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:18.330
he marched up those steps and
called our king a halfwit,

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:19.780
compared His Grace to the Mad King

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:21.713
and suggested he'd meet the same fate.

00:06:21.713 --> 00:06:24.180
When I spoke in the king's defense,

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:26.250
he threatened to have me killed.

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:29.360
- Okay, so you might assume
that these statements

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:33.300
by Tyrion qualify as hearsay and therefore

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:36.910
can't be used against
Tyrion in a court of law.

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:38.580
However, it's important to know

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.060
that hearsay is an
out-of-court statement used

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:43.370
for the truth of the matter asserted.

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:45.080
They are trying to
establish that he actually

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.630
said these things and had a
motive to kill King Joffrey.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:51.270
So that is sort of the truth
of the statement at issue,

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:55.980
that he thought the king was
a vicious idiot and a fool.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.750
So the question is, can
those statements come in?

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.810
Well, one of the exceptions
to the hearsay rule is

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:05.160
generally the admission
by a party opponent

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:08.050
or a party admission
exception to the hearsay rule.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:12.230
And under that rule, you
are allowed to use hearsay

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:16.730
statements where the statement
is ostensibly made by a party

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:18.680
to the litigation or
a party, in this case,

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:22.380
to the criminal proceeding,
and is adverse to that party.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.210
And here they are using
a statement of Tyrion's,

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:26.450
who is a party to the litigation.

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:29.750
He is the defendant, and it
is adverse to his interests.

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:31.800
So that statement is allowed to come in.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:35.600
Generally, the admission
by a party exception exists

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:39.210
because you can
cross-examine the declarant

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:41.920
because they're a party
to the proceedings.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:44.440
The big problem, at least historically,

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:46.290
with hearsay is that it's
an out-of-court statement

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:48.760
where you could not
cross-examine the declarant.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:50.870
But where the declarant is available,

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:52.950
you are allowed to bring in their

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:55.880
out-of-court statements
through other witnesses.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:59.030
So in this particular case,
I think the party admission

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:01.510
exception would apply,
and they would be able

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:05.151
to use Tyrion's statements
against him in this proceeding.

00:08:05.151 --> 00:08:08.020
- Oh, why don't you tell
them what Joffrey was doing?

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.360
- Silence!
- Pointing a loaded crossbow

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.960
at Sansa Stark while you tore
at her clothes and beat her.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:13.370
- Silence!

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:15.670
- That would probably be
another constitutional

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:17.570
violation in the United States.

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:20.800
Tyrion has a right to
confront his accusers.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.790
And although he's probably not allowed

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:25.530
to do it during the direct
examination of the witness,

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:27.520
eventually he would have the right

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:31.040
to cross-examine the person who
was making these statements,

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:35.150
especially when that person
is relaying statements

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:37.590
that Tyrion supposedly made out of court.

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:40.110
So for him to be shut down entirely

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:41.950
and not be able to face his accusers

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:43.700
and the witnesses against him would be

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:45.810
a clear US constitutional violation.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:47.700
The confrontation clause
to the Sixth Amendment

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.630
states that in all criminal prosecutions,

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:51.610
the accused shall enjoy the right

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:54.310
to be confronted with the
witnesses against him.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:56.460
And here Tyrion does not have the right

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:58.560
to confront the witnesses
against him, and that would be

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.820
a clear constitutional violation
in this criminal trial.

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:03.680
- This was found on the body

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:06.770
of Dontos Hollard, the king's fool.

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:10.800
He was last seen spiriting Sansa Stark,

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:14.263
the wife of the accused,
away from the feast.

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:19.503
She wore this necklace
the day of the wedding.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:23.610
Residue of the most rare

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:27.563
and terrible poison was found inside.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.730
- Was this one of the poisons
stolen from your store?

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:34.281
- It was the strangler,

00:09:34.281 --> 00:09:35.749
(audience gasps)

00:09:35.749 --> 00:09:38.590
a poison few in the Seven Kingdoms possess

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:43.010
and used to strike down
the most noble child

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:46.210
the gods ever put on this good Earth.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:48.410
- Yes, King Joffrey, what a good guy.

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:51.830
Such a shame that he
perished in his youth.

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:55.040
Of course, this is completely
improper character evidence,

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:56.640
which is prejudicial against the

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:57.990
accused in this particular case.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:09:59.700
But that is why character evidence

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:01.790
should not be used in
a criminal proceeding.

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.540
Putting aside the problems
with the Grand Maester's

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:07.290
expert opinion here, in
any criminal proceeding,

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:09.460
you have to show what's
called the chain of custody

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:11.160
of any particular piece of evidence.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:13.270
Because the police will
gather the evidence,

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.760
and then you need to show what happens

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:18.570
to that evidence from every
step, from being gathered

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:21.510
to being stored to
being produced in court,

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:23.610
because all kinds of things can go wrong,

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:25.310
especially when you're dealing with

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:27.760
DNA evidence or fingerprint evidence.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:30.680
And here, the Grand Maester has apparently

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:33.170
just picked up this random bracelet.

00:10:33.170 --> 00:10:36.210
He then stuffed it into his shirt pocket

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:38.760
or the sleeve of his shirt and just

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.810
withdrew it at the trial itself.

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:44.670
If I was a criminal
defendant, I would really want

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:48.300
to know what happened and
who possessed this necklace

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:50.740
or this bracelet throughout
the entire course

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:53.100
of its existence so that I could show

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:55.200
that it hadn't been tampered with.

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:57.520
If the Grand Maester, for example,

00:10:57.520 --> 00:10:59.620
had an ax to grind against the defendant,

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:02.270
which he clearly does
here, he could've taken

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:05.160
the bracelet and added
a drop of the poison

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:10.160
that he is accused of using
against Joffrey the King here.

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.230
So there's all kinds of
reasons that in modern-day

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:15.980
jurisprudence you have
to show chain of custody.

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:18.800
And if I was able to
cross-examine this Grand Maester,

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:21.510
I think I could really
do a world of damage

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:24.510
showing his, number one,
bias, and number two,

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:26.420
that the chain of custody of effectively

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:29.800
the murder weapon is really
questionable at best.

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:33.320
- [Tywin] Do you remember the
precise nature of this threat?

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:35.150
- I'm afraid I do, my lord.

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:38.597
He said, "Perhaps you should
speak more softly to me, then.

00:11:38.597 --> 00:11:41.237
"Monsters are dangerous, and just now

00:11:41.237 --> 00:11:43.419
"kings are dying like flies."

00:11:43.419 --> 00:11:44.252
- [Tywin] And he said this to you

00:11:44.252 --> 00:11:46.420
at a meeting of the small council?

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:50.830
- Yes, after we received
word of Robb Stark's death.

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:53.460
He didn't seem gladdened by the news.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:55.470
Perhaps his marriage to Sansa Stark

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:58.144
had made him more sympathetic
to the northern cause.

00:11:58.144 --> 00:11:59.870
(audience gasps and groans)

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:02.030
- While the latter
portion of that testimony

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:05.220
is clearly speculation,
he's speculating about

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:07.550
the frame of mind that Tyrion had

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:10.140
when he received the news
of Robb Stark's death,

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:13.630
the prior statements of Tyrion
at the small council meeting,

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:16.850
again, probably come in
under the party admission

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:19.210
exception to the hearsay rule.

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:22.353
- Father, may I ask the
witness one question?

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:30.690
- One.

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:33.730
- Finally Tyrion gets to
confront one of his accusers.

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:36.380
Geez, this is clearly a
Sixth Amendment violation.

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:38.170
And on top of it, no judge in the world

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:40.930
would ever limit a criminal defendant

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:43.690
facing life imprisonment or execution

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:47.010
to one single question
on cross-examination.

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:49.740
This is an abomination
of a criminal trial,

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:52.350
at least as it accords with the

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:54.953
US modern standards of jurisprudence.

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:58.360
It's not good to be a criminal defendant

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:03.360
in 15th century England, or
the equivalent in Westeros.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:08.450
- I did not kill Joffrey,
but I wish that I had.

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:11.270
Watching your vicious bastard die

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:15.396
gave me more relief than
a thousand lying whores.

00:13:15.396 --> 00:13:18.800
(dramatic orchestral music)
(audience jeers)

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:22.773
I wish I was the monster you think I am.

00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:27.960
I wish I had enough poison
for the whole pack of you.

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:31.233
I would gladly give my life
to watch you all swallow it.

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:34.180
- I haven't seen that in court,

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:36.660
but generally that's not a great strategy.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:39.360
Not a great defense strategy to insult

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:43.460
the entire gallery and the
entire jury at the same time

00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:45.390
and suggest that although you didn't

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:48.270
kill the victim, you wish you had.

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:52.940
- I will not give my life
for Joffrey's murder,

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:55.860
and I know I'll get no justice here.

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.625
So I will let the gods decide my fate.

00:13:58.625 --> 00:14:00.800
(suspenseful orchestral music)

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:03.693
I demand a trial by combat.

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:07.420
- I assume you'd have to
make some sort of motion

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:11.150
with a court and you can't
just request a trial by combat

00:14:12.549 --> 00:14:14.930
when the trial is not going
particularly well for you.

00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:17.360
That being said, trial
by combat is a real thing

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:21.410
that actually existed in
the 15th century in England.

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:23.140
But it also existed well into

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:24.940
the 20th century in many places.

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:26.970
And in fact, some of the last instances

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:31.220
of trial by combat existed
in the 1960s in France.

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:33.550
So it is not crazy for a criminal

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:35.480
defendant to request trial by combat.

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:38.270
Probably not a great move by Tyrion,

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:39.990
because I think generally speaking,

00:14:39.990 --> 00:14:42.070
real-life trial by combat required

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:45.280
the actual accused to be
the one doing the combat.

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.130
But trial by combat was never

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:48.700
an option in the United States,

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:51.834
so not realistic from a US perspective.

00:14:51.834 --> 00:14:54.501
(playful music)

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:58.370
Okay, that was the trial
of Tyrion Lannister

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:00.730
from season four of Game of Thrones.

00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:02.360
Now it's time to give this episode

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:04.166
a grade for legal realism.

00:15:04.166 --> 00:15:06.730
(gavel bangs)

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:09.180
On the one hand, you have
evidence that is certainly

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.850
probative of someone
actually committing regicide

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:13.900
in killing the king through poison.

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:15.250
You have some hearsay statements

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:17.510
that are likely to come in under

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:19.940
the exception to the hearsay rule,

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:22.170
so that evidence probably would've been

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:24.310
able to be used in this particular trial.

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:25.650
On the other hand, you have tons

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:29.000
of constitutional violations,
including the violation

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:30.900
of the right against self-incrimination,

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:34.140
the violation of being able
to confront your accused,

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:35.720
and the violation of having an attorney

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:37.640
present during a criminal proceeding.

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:39.670
All in all, Tyrion did
not get a fair trial

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:41.220
and the deck was stacked against him

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:45.230
in a way that hopefully does
not exist in modern US law.

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:47.670
So I'm giving the trial
of Tyrion Lannister

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:49.950
a D-minus for accuracy.

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:51.210
This episode should be killed

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.680
by poison, preferably the strangler.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:56.120
- I found it surprisingly beautiful

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:59.920
in a brutal, horribly
uncomfortable sort of way.

